!
! EFG finite field calculations on hydrogen fluoride with a small basis set.
!
| The run zerofield serves to generate the MO-vectors and gives the EFG
! expectation value at the DHF & MP2 level. The finite field approach 
! for the CCSD(T) value can be done in two ways :
!
! 1 : Using relaxed orbitals
!     - Use the inputs scf.ccsd.1 and scf.ccsd.m1 (and other field strenghts)
!       to reoptimize the orbitals in the presence of an arbitrary (strong)
!       quadrupole field at one of the nuclei. The obtained total CCSD(T)
!       energies can be used to obtain the EFG by numerical differentation.
!
! 2 : Using fixed orbitals
!     - Use the inputs ccsd.1 and ccsd.m1 (and other field strenghts)
!       and send in the orbitals from the zero-field reference calculation
!       to only obtain the matrix elements in the unchanged MO-basis in the
!       presence of the field. Run the CCSD(T) and you have a reasonable 
!       approximation to the more rigorous result of 1. In cases where one 
!       uses high exponents in the basis this method gives less numerical
!       noise and is to be preferred.
!       Note that the costly 4-index cases needs to be performed only once
!       if you save the file MDCINT and uncomment the .NO4IND line.
!
! Results :
! 
! DHF analytic (calculated via property module as exp. val.) 2.728 au
! DHF fin. diff. (via relaxed orb. results)                  2.727 au
! DHF fin. diff. (via fixed orb. results)                    2.728 au
!
! MP2 analytic   (via CCFOPR module in RELCCSD)              2.608 au
! MP2 fin. diff. (via relaxed orb. results)                  2.609 au
! MP2 fin. diff. (via fixed orb.results)                     2.754 au
!
! CCSD(T) fin. diff. (via relaxed orb. results)              2.670 au
! CCSD(T) fin. diff. (via fixed orb. results)                2.672 au
!
! Notes: - the MP2 unrelaxed fin. diff. approach gives unreliable results !
!        - the relaxed and unrelaxed CCSD(T) results are very close
!        - the MP2 method overshoots the correlation correction by a factor of 2
